00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:02.399 align:start position:0%
welcome to the daily climate show coming  up  seven leaders meet in germany amid fears  the climate crisis has been knocked off  the global agenda by the war in ukraine  a new report finds biodiversity could be  damaged beyond repair if the world  doesn't stick to temperature rise  targets  and climate protesters take to the  streets of sydney as australia reveals  the greenhouse gas emissions are still  rising there  world leaders have gathered in germany  for the annual g7 summit but unlike last  year's meeting in cornwall climate  change is not top of the agenda with  russia's invasion of ukraine being the  focus leaders have however spoken of  their continued commitment to the paris  agreement and the glasgow climate pact  aiming to limit the global temperature  rises and reduce the use of fossil fuels  well our europe correspondent siobhan  robbins is at the summit and joins us  live from bavaria  siobhan  why is climate change being overshadowed  so much this year obviously the war but  it's still being talked about  it is still being talked about but as  you say there's been a massive shift  from when this conference was first  planned so when germany announced this  conference it was highly focused on  climate and then russia invaded ukraine  and the focus has switched to the war  that doesn't mean that climate isn't on  the agenda it certainly is along with  other linked uh conversations like  energy security and food shortages but  it has been overshadowed somewhat uh by  the war in ukraine and so today we saw  the g7 countries along with partners who  have been invited here including india  and indonesia reaffirming their  commitments to the paris agreement  saying that they would continue to try  and phase down coal also trying to cut  carbon but i think there are a lot of  questions around some of the statements  that are coming out that one in  particular seems very thin on  information particularly if you  remember the fact that it was india that  was one of the countries that led to  that dilution of the wording at glasgow  around coal so it was meant to be phase  out coal it was then diluted to phase  down coal so questions there and also  actually questions around germany itself  because since the war in ukraine we all  know there's been a big squeeze on  energy supplies and ru and germany was  highly dependent on russia for both gas  and oil now russia has been reducing the  gas it's sending to germany to the point  where a level two gas alert has been  announced here and because of that this  country has started to have to lean back  on coal to help fill the gap but also is  looking at exploring new natural gas and  that's led to criticism of the  chancellor who's hosting this meeting  now he's saying they will still meet  their climate commitments but i've been  speaking to protesters who've been here  every single day of this summit and many  of them are very concerned that climate  justice and climate change have really  slipped down the agenda which cannot be  afforded when it's the world's leading  powers holding these meetings  siobhan robbins thanks for that our  europe correspondent live in bavaria for  us at the g7 now let's get some more of  the day's climate news the japanese  government is asking residents in tokyo  to conserve energy as it warns of  possible power shortages the country is  currently experiencing unusually hot  temperatures with officials saying this  has been the earliest end to the annual  rainy season since records began in  1951.  according to local reports more than 250  people were taken to hospital over the  weekend with heat stroke  10 people have been arrested after at  least 100 climate change protesters  caused chaos across sydney's city center  centre blockade australia members ran  into busy roads and tried to stop peak  hour traffic by dragging bins and  barriers into the middle of the street  the group say they're trying to raise  awareness about climate change and are  calling for further action by leaders  well it comes as australia recorded an  increase in greenhouse gas emissions  last year the country released 488  million metric tons of carbon dioxide  and other gases last year that's up 0.8  from 2020. transport emissions rose 4  and those from agricultural production  also increased australia's newly elected  government pinned the blame on the  previous administration and its inaction  on climate change  now overshooting global temperature rise  targets is likely to cause major and  often irreversible damage to the natural  world that's the finding from a new  study conducted by the university of  cape town and university college london  in 2015 global leaders set a goal of  limiting global warming to well below a  rise of two degrees celsius above  pre-industrial levels but those targets  are just not being met  you can see from the estimate here that  we're already well over one degree of  temperature rises and they could go up  by as much as three and a half degrees  celsius by the end of the century unless  more action is taken to lower emissions  and this new study finds that exceeding  just two degrees is likely to have huge  impacts for biodiversity which will be  difficult to reverse even if  temperatures do eventually decline well  joining us now is one of the reports  authors dr christopher trisos from the  university of cape town dr  thank you very much for joining us could  you describe to us a little bit more  about the actual impact of these  temperature rises on plants and animals  etc  yes thank you for having me so our  research shows that although in an  overshoot scenario people talk about  global heating being reversible so  because of delayed emissions greenhouse  emissions gas cuts global heating would  go above that two degrees threshold and  then carbon dioxide removal technologies  would be used to reverse that  temperature rise so even though the  temperature rise might be reversible in  many places the biodiversity losses  could be permanent such as species going  extinct  so our study looked at communities of  animal species on land and in the oceans  across the earth and found that in many  regions risks to biodiversity from a  global heating overshoot could last  twice as long as the overshoot period  itself so in the scenario we looked at  we would be above 2 degrees celsius for  around 60 years but it would take over a  century  for those pre-overshoot conditions to  return for biodiversity and for almost a  third of animal communities it's  uncertain whether their exposure to  dangerous temperatures would go away  even by the year 2300  and for many ecosystems like coral reefs  or the amazon which is a crucial carbon  storehouse for the planet  this would be expected to lead to  irreversible species losses and  ecosystem degradation  and why will it take species so long to  recover from these temperature rises  so the answer is that for many species  they're very sensitive to temperatures  such as in the tropics they live close  to their thermal thresholds and also in  many parts of the the planet the climate  change that's local is at a different  rate from the global climate change so  when we talk about an overshoot scenario  what policymakers are talking about is  the temperature would go up above some  government  global government agreed threshold like  two degrees celsius and then come back  down but at local scales that return of  temperature might be much slower than  the global average and so for places  like the tropics where many species are  very sensitive to temperatures and these  temperatures could take a long time to  come back down this is a very high risk  strategy so even though we might talk  about overshoot scenarios as bringing  temperatures back down to safe levels or  less dangerous levels what this research  is showing is the journey to get there  could still be very dangerous for  thousands of species globally  dr trisos thank you so much for joining  us today  that is all from us unfortunately you've  been watching the daily climate show  back to you sarah jane
 

